<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emilio Aguinaldo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emilio Aguinaldo was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from 1896. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-biak-na-bato">Pact of Biak-na-Bato</a> he went into exile, returned in 1898 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> and declared independence on 12 June 1898. He became president of the First Philippine Republic in 1899 and led resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> until his capture in 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo.jpg" alt="Emilio Aguinaldo" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing Photo Studio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emilio_Aguinaldo_ca._1919_(Restored).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b>, head of the Dictatorial Government of the Philippines, commissioned it on 5 June 1898 for the proclamation of independence from Spain, to replace a march he had found unsatisfactory, and gave the task to Julián Felipe.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang (Chosen Land)</a></li><li><blockquote>In northern Luzon, a 60-man Filipino rearguard under Brigadier General Gregorio del Pilar was overcome by more than 500 Americans, but delayed their advance long enough for President <b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b> and his troops to escape.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The federal state was headed by Roque Lopez as president, with a council representing islands such as Panay and Negros, and it acknowledged the authority of <b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-state-of-the-visayas">Federal State of the Visayas</a></li><li><blockquote>Following the Revolutionary Government of the Philippines and the drafting of the Malolos Constitution by a congress of revolutionary delegates, the Philippine Republic was inaugurated at Barasoain church in Malolos on 23 January 1899, with <b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b> as president and Apolinario Mabini as head of the cabinet.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-philippine-republic">First Philippine Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>After Spanish offensives drove the revolutionaries from Cavite in 1897, <b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b> retreated to the caves of Biak-na-Bato in San Miguel, Bulacan.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-biak-na-bato">Republic of Biak-na-Bato</a></li><li><blockquote>After the United States destroyed the Spanish fleet at Manila Bay on 1 May 1898, <b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b> returned from exile in Hong Kong and on 24 May proclaimed a dictatorial government at Cavite.</blockquote><a href="/state/revolutionary-government-of-the-philippines">Revolutionary Government of the Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
